Looking at this problem, I need to find the minimum segment length *n* such that for **any** circular arrangement of the 2014 packets (106 signatures × 19 copies each), there **exists** a contiguous segment of length *n* containing at least 53 distinct signatures.

## Setting Up the Counting Argument

Let me define, for each signature *s*, the quantity *f(s)* = number of starting positions (out of 2014) for which a segment of length *n* contains at least one packet with signature *s*.

The total **T** = Σ_s *f(s)* equals the sum over all 2014 segments of (number of distinct signatures in that segment).

**Lower bound on *f(s)*:** The 19 packets of signature *s* sit at some positions on the circle. The segments capturing at least one of them form the union of 19 intervals of length *n*. This union is minimized when the 19 packets are **consecutive**, giving a single interval of length *n* + 18. Therefore:

$$f(s) \geq n + 18 \quad \text{for every signature } s$$

$$T \geq 106(n + 18)$$

**Upper bound if every segment has ≤ 52 distinct signatures:**

$$T \leq 52 \times 2014$$

## Deriving the Threshold

Combining: if every segment of length *n* has at most 52 distinct signatures, then:

$$106(n + 18) \leq 52 \times 2014$$

Since 2014 = 106 × 19:

$$n + 18 \leq 52 \times 19 = 988 \implies n \leq 970$$

**For *n* = 971:** 106 × 989 = 104,834 > 104,728 = 52 × 2014. Contradiction — so some segment must have ≥ 53 distinct signatures. ✓

**For *n* = 970:** 106 × 988 = 104,728 = 52 × 2014. Equality is consistent — it's possible that every segment has exactly 52 distinct signatures.

## Constructing the Tight Arrangement (n = 970 fails)

**Clustered arrangement:** Place all 19 copies of each signature consecutively, forming 106 blocks of 19 around the circle:

$$[\underbrace{s_1 \cdots s_1}_{19}][\underbrace{s_2 \cdots s_2}_{19}]\cdots[\underbrace{s_{106} \cdots s_{106}}_{19}]$$

A segment of length 970 starting at position *a* within a block (1 ≤ *a* ≤ 19) covers:
- (20 − *a*) packets of the current block, then ⌊(950 + *a*) / 19⌋ full blocks, then a remainder.

Checking all cases:
- *a* = 1: 19 + 50×19 + 1 = 970 → touches 1 + 50 + 1 = **52 blocks**
- *a* = 19: 1 + 51×19 + 0 = 970 → touches 1 + 51 = **52 blocks**
- Every *a* yields exactly **52 blocks touched** = 52 distinct signatures.

So **no segment of length 970 reaches 53 distinct signatures** in this arrangement. ✓

For *n* = 971: starting at *a* = 19 (last packet of a block), the segment covers 1 + 51×19 + 1 = 971 packets, touching 1 + 51 + 1 = **53 blocks** = 53 distinct signatures. ✓

## Verification of Tightness

The equality 106 × 988 = 52 × 2014 holds because:

$$106 \times 988 = 106 \times 52 \times 19 = 52 \times (106 \times 19) = 52 \times 2014 \checkmark$$

This confirms the counting bound is **exactly tight**: the clustered arrangement achieves equality, with each *f(s)* = 988 and each segment having exactly 52 distinct signatures.

## Conclusion

- **n = 970 is insufficient**: the clustered arrangement is a counterexample (every length-970 segment has only 52 distinct signatures).
- **n = 971 is sufficient**: the counting/pigeonhole argument forces at least one segment of length 971 to contain ≥ 53 distinct signatures, for any arrangement.

$$\boxed{n = 971}$$

